Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action adventure. All animals are naturalistic and non-speaking.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

The sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley, 1897. A wide veranda overlooks lawns, orchards, stables, and a cement swimming tank.

BUCK, a four-year-old, one-hundred-and-forty-pound dog, rules the estate. He has the massive chest of a St. Bernard, the long muzzle of a Scotch shepherd, a brown head and back, and a white blaze down his chest.

Buck plunges into the tank, carries a laughing child across the lawn, then settles with stately dignity beside JUDGE MILLER.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was neither house-dog nor kennel-dog. The whole realm was his. He was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

Beyond the trees, MANUEL watches Buck. He checks a Chinese lottery ticket, then fingers his empty purse.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

Manuel leads Buck from the orchard toward a waiting STRANGER, ELIAS HOLT, a lean laborer in a battered coat.

Coins chink into Manuel's hand.

ELIAS HOLT
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles a stout rope around Buck's neck.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Manuel leaves. Buck stares after him, puzzled.

Elias tightens the rope. Buck springs, but Elias grapples him, throws him onto his back, and chokes him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never in all his life had he been so vilely treated, and never had he been so angry.

Buck's struggles weaken. A locomotive screams into the station.

Elias and a BAGGAGEMAN named ARTHUR PIKE throw Buck into the baggage car.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck wakes amid swaying crates. His throat is swollen, his eyes bloodshot.

Elias reaches for him. Buck's jaws close on Elias's hand.

The rope jerks tight. Darkness swallows Buck again.

ELIAS HOLT
Yep, has fits. I'm takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco.

EXT. SEATTLE DOG YARD - DAY

A high-walled yard. Buck's crate lies splintered beneath an axe.

A stout RED-SWEATERED KEEPER named EDWARD BLAKE stands before the opening with a heavy club.

Buck launches out. The club catches him in midair and smashes him down.

He rises and charges again.

Another blow. Another fall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had never been struck by a club in his life. A dozen times he charged, and as often the club broke the charge.

Buck staggers upright, blood running from his nose and mouth.

Edward delivers the final calculated blow. Buck collapses.

Later, Buck wakes beside a bowl of water. Edward crouches nearby.

EDWARD BLAKE
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck bristles when Edward pats him, but does not attack. He drinks, then takes raw meat from Edward's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

PERRAULT, a small, weathered French-Canadian government courier, inspects Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog. How moch?

EDWARD BLAKE
Three hundred, and a present at that.

Money changes hands.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

A steamer rests beside the frozen Dyea shore.

PERRAULT and FRANCOIS, a powerful, dark-skinned French-Canadian dog-driver in fur and canvas, lead Buck down the gangway.

With them are CURLY, a friendly black Newfoundland; SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with cold blue eyes; and DAVE, a lean, morose gray sled dog.

Buck steps into fresh snow. His paws sink.

He leaps backward, snorts, then catches a flake on his tongue. It vanishes.

Perrault and Francois laugh.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA CAMP - DAY

Tents, sleds, axes, shouting men, and scores of wolfish dogs crowd the beach.

Curly approaches a large HUSKY. The husky slashes her face and leaps away.

Curly lunges. The husky strikes her chest and tumbles her off her feet.

A silent ring of dogs closes around her.

Buck surges forward, but Francois restrains him. The pack descends. Curly's scream ends beneath snapping bodies.

Francois swings an axe and scatters them. Curly lies dead in the trampled snow.

Spitz watches Buck and runs out his scarlet tongue in a silent laugh.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Buck would see to it that he never went down.

Francois fastens Buck into a sled harness between Dave and Spitz.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

The team starts. Buck tangles the traces. Dave nips his hind leg while Spitz jerks him into line.

FRANCOIS
Ho!

The team stops.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Buck lunges with the others. This time the traces remain straight.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

Buck lies shivering beside the glowing tent. Perrault throws a pan at him when he tries to enter.

Buck circles through the snow. His forelegs break into a hollow.

BILLEE, a small, gentle husky, lies curled beneath the snow and gives a friendly yelp.

Buck digs his own hollow, curls tightly, and disappears beneath drifting powder.

At dawn, Buck bursts from the snow in a glittering cloud.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

The team hauls through deep drifts and over blue ice.

Buck gulps his ration before the other dogs can steal it. When Perrault turns away, Buck steals a strip of bacon and swallows it whole.

DUB, an awkward brown husky, is caught near the empty sack and receives Perrault's scolding.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This first theft marked Buck as fit to survive. He stole secretly and cunningly, out of respect for club and fang.

Buck's paws harden. His shoulders thicken. At night, he points his muzzle toward the stars and howls.

A distant chorus answers him.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Driving snow sweeps across the frozen lake. A driftwood fire burns beneath a rock wall.

Buck returns from his meal and finds Spitz occupying his warm nest.

Spitz growls.

Buck explodes into him. They roll through the snow, jaws flashing.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

Starving huskies surge from the darkness and overturn the food box.

Perrault and Francois swing clubs while Buck's team fights the gaunt invaders.

Spitz darts in from the side and bites Buck's throat.

Buck tears free, memorizing the treachery. The team flees across the lake.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit races beneath the moon.

Buck leads a pack of sled dogs after it, body stretched in exultant leaps. Spitz cuts across a bend and kills the rabbit.

Buck drives into Spitz without slowing.

They circle in a silent ring of watching dogs.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and springs clear. Buck rushes again, seeking the white throat.

Fang clashes against fang.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spitz was a practised fighter. Buck fought by instinct, but he could fight by head as well.

Blood darkens Buck's chest. Spitz rushes and nearly overturns him.

Buck feints high, sweeps low, and closes his jaws on Spitz's left foreleg.

A bone cracks.

Spitz struggles on three legs. Buck repeats the feint and breaks the right foreleg.

The watching circle tightens.

Spitz quivers, unable to stand. Buck strikes him shoulder to shoulder.

Spitz falls beneath the pack and disappears.

Buck stands in the moonlight, bleeding and triumphant.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The successful champion. The dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Francois examines Buck's wounds. Spitz is gone.

FRANCOIS
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

PERRAULT
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells.

Francois places SOL-LEKS, an old one-eyed husky, in the lead position.

Buck drives Sol-leks aside and takes the place.

Francois advances with a club. Buck retreats beyond its reach but refuses his old position.

Perrault checks his watch. Francois throws down the club and moves Sol-leks back.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Buck trots forward and allows himself to be harnessed as leader.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

The team surges ahead as one.

Buck corrects a shirking dog with a snap, guides the sled around broken ice, and drives the team faster.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

The sled races beneath the aurora.

EXT. SKAGUAY CAMP - DAY

Buck and his team stand gaunt and exhausted after thousands of trail miles.

HAL, nineteen, wears a cartridge belt, revolver, and hunting knife. CHARLES, middle-aged and stiff, lashes a mountain of baggage onto a sled. MERCEDES, Charles's soft, well-dressed wife and Hal's sister, rearranges every bundle.

MERCEDES
However in the world could I manage without a tent?

HAL
Mush on there!

The dogs strain. The frozen sled does not move.

Hal lashes them.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't! The poor dears!

A seasoned musher, SAMUEL KEENE, watches from a neighboring tent.

SAMUEL KEENE
They're weak as water. Plum tuckered out. They need a rest.

HAL
Rest be blanked.

Samuel points to the runners.

SAMUEL KEENE
You can help them by breaking out that sled. The runners are froze fast.

Hal wrenches the sled loose. The dogs stagger forward.

At the first turn, the top-heavy sled overturns. Hal falls, the sled rolls over him, and the dogs scatter baggage down the street.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Spring sunlight glitters on thin, rotting river ice.

Only five skeletal dogs remain. Buck lies at their head, his ribs visible beneath a blood-matted coat.

JOHN THORNTON, a broad, weathered prospector with kind eyes and a slight limp, whittles an axe handle beside his tent.

HAL
They told us the bottom was dropping out of the trail. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
And they told you true. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal raises his whip.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

The other dogs crawl upright. Buck does not move.

The whip strikes him again and again. Thornton's jaw tightens.

Hal trades the whip for a club. The club pounds Buck's failing body.

Thornton springs up and hurls Hal backward.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Hal draws his hunting knife.

Thornton knocks it away with the axe handle, then cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes depart with the remaining dogs. Mercedes rides atop the loaded sled.

Thornton kneels beside Buck.

A quarter-mile away, the rear of the sled drops. Hal clings to the rising gee-pole.

MERCEDES
Hal!

The ice collapses. Dogs, sled, Hal, Charles, and Mercedes vanish into the black river.

Buck and Thornton stare at the yawning hole.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Weeks later. Buck's coat shines again, his muscles restored.

SKEET, a small red Irish setter, cleans his last healing wound. NIG, a huge black dog with laughing eyes, rests nearby.

Thornton takes Buck's head between his hands and shakes him affectionately.

JOHN THORNTON
You great, foolish brute.

Buck gently closes his jaws around Thornton's hand, then releases it.

Thornton laughs.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck lies at Thornton's feet, studying his face.

From the forest comes a faint, distant wolf howl.

Buck raises his head. He takes one step toward the trees, then looks back at Thornton and returns to the fire.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Deep in the forest a call was sounding. But as often as he heard it, the love for John Thornton drew him back again.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

A crowd surrounds a freight sled loaded with twenty sacks of flour. Its runners are frozen to the packed snow.

MATTHEWSON, a wealthy miner in an expensive fur coat, drops a sack of gold before Thornton.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JOHN THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds, break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

Buck is harnessed alone.

Thornton kneels, presses cheek to cheek with him, and whispers.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck closes his jaws around Thornton's mittened hand, then lets go.

Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right. The runners crack in their frozen bed.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. The sled pivots several inches.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck throws himself forward. His chest nearly touches the snow. Muscles writhe beneath his coat.

The sled trembles.

One paw slips. Buck catches himself.

The runners lurch forward, inch by inch, then glide steadily.

Thornton runs beside him.

Buck passes the hundred-yard marker. The crowd erupts.

Thornton falls to his knees and embraces him.

MATTHEWSON
I'll give you twelve hundred for him.

Thornton rises, tears on his face.

JOHN THORNTON
No, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. GOLD VALLEY CAMP - DAY

A remote wilderness valley. Moose-hide sacks of gold stand beside a spruce-bough lodge.

Thornton, PETE, a bearded frontiersman, and HANS, a compact German woodsman, wash gold from a shallow placer.

Buck watches from the forest edge, larger and more wolf-like than ever.

JOHN THORNTON
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

Buck slips beneath the trees.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

A long, lean TIMBER WOLF sits beneath the moon and howls.

Buck emerges, half-crouched. The wolf flees.

Buck overtakes and corners him beside a fallen tree, but does not attack. He circles with cautious, friendly advances.

At last, they touch noses.

They run side by side through the forest until dawn, crossing streams and climbing a divide.

At a running brook, Buck stops.

The wolf urges him onward. Buck looks back toward the distant camp.

He turns home.

The wolf follows for a time, then sits and gives a mournful howl as Buck disappears.

EXT. GOLD VALLEY CAMP - DAY

Buck bursts from the trees and throws himself upon Thornton, licking his face and gripping his hand in a fierce caress.

Thornton laughs beneath him.

JOHN THORNTON
Playing the general tom-fool, are you?

Thornton shakes Buck lovingly.

Days pass. Buck ranges farther from camp.

He stalks and brings down a massive bull moose after a long pursuit, then feeds beside the carcass.

A strange scent reaches him.

Buck raises his bloodied muzzle. His mane bristles.

He races toward camp.

Near the clearing, Buck finds Nig dead with an arrow through his body.

Hans lies face-down, feathered with arrows.

A chant sounds ahead.

Buck crawls forward.

EXT. GOLD VALLEY CAMP - DAY

YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wrecked lodge.

Buck erupts into the clearing with a roar.

He tears the throat of CHIEF KITCHI, then strikes WARRIOR MAKWA before either can raise a weapon.

Arrows fly. The hunters hit one another as Buck moves among them too quickly to target.

The surviving Yeehats flee into the forest.

Buck pursues, dragging one down, then returns to the silent camp.

Pete lies dead in his blankets. Skeet lies beside a muddy pool.

Buck follows Thornton's scent to the water. No trail comes out.

He stands chest-deep in the pool, searching.

Silence.

Buck climbs onto the bank and waits beside the water until moonrise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. GOLD VALLEY CLEARING - NIGHT

A wolf pack pours into the moonlit clearing.

Buck waits motionless.

The boldest wolf attacks. Buck strikes and breaks its neck.

Three more rush him. He wheels, slashes, and drives them back.

The pack surrounds him until the long, lean timber wolf steps forward.

Buck recognizes his wild brother.

They touch noses.

An OLD WOLF advances. Buck stiffens, then accepts his scent.

The old wolf sits, lifts his muzzle, and howls.

The pack joins him.

Buck sits and adds his deep voice to the song.

The wolves spring into the forest. Buck runs with them, side by side with his wild brother.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And here may well end the story of Buck.

Years pass in the pale moonlight.

A great wolf pack races through the valley. At its head runs Buck, gigantic above his fellows, brown across the head and muzzle, with a white rift down his chest.

He pauses beside the overgrown pool where yellow dust seeps from rotted moose-hide sacks.

Buck howls once, long and mournfully.

Then he turns and rejoins the pack, singing the song of the younger world.

FADE OUT.

THE END
